---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Docs | Z Best Media"
  description="Documentation overview for Z Best Media operations, governance contracts, security controls, runbooks, and implementation guidance."
  path="/docs"
>
  <section class="grid gap-5">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight">Docs</h1>
    <p class="text-muted max-w-[75ch]">
      Documentation is a control surface, not a formality. Z Best Media documentation is structured so operators can execute reliably,
      leaders can evaluate posture quickly, and auditors can validate evidence without ambiguity. The docs model links architecture,
      contracts, runtime behavior, and response procedures into one coherent system. This page outlines what teams can expect from the
      documentation stack and how it supports day-to-day operations, incident readiness, and governance transparency.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10 grid gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Architecture and Data Model Documentation</h2>
    <p class="text-muted max-w-[75ch]">
      Core architecture docs explain service boundaries, event flows, dependency constraints, and data contracts with enough precision for
      engineering and operations teams to align quickly. Every critical surface is documented with intent, input assumptions, output
      guarantees, and failure semantics. Data model documentation includes schema ownership, evolution rules, and compatibility guidance so
      downstream systems can integrate safely. This lowers onboarding cost for new team members and reduces errors caused by implicit
      assumptions that never made it into formal records.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10 grid gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Governance Contracts and Policy Specs</h2>
    <p class="text-muted max-w-[75ch]">
      Governance docs define the enforceable rules that shape runtime behavior. Teams can review signed contract expectations, threshold
      definitions, escalation logic, and enforcement modes in plain language with technical references attached. Changes are versioned so
      policy drift can be identified and reviewed before it impacts production. This gives legal, compliance, and operations functions a
      shared interpretation layer and reduces disagreement during high-pressure reviews.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10 grid gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Runbooks and Incident Procedures</h2>
    <p class="text-muted max-w-[75ch]">
      Runbooks are designed for real incidents where time and clarity matter. Each runbook includes trigger conditions, containment
      actions, decision checkpoints, communication requirements, and post-incident evidence capture. Procedures are mapped to system
      capabilities like freeze actions, rollback paths, and audit exports so teams can execute directly instead of improvising. This
      approach improves consistency across responders and shortens time from alert to coordinated action.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10 grid gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Operational Metrics and Reporting Guides</h2>
    <p class="text-muted max-w-[75ch]">
      Metrics documentation defines what is measured, why it matters, and how to interpret trend changes. Teams can tie technical
      indicators to business and governance outcomes, making reporting useful for executives as well as operators. Definitions for score
      thresholds, alert severity, and review cadences keep analytics consistent across teams. This prevents conflicting narratives that
      emerge when departments use different metric logic for the same system behavior.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10 grid gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Implementation and Adoption Playbook</h2>
    <p class="text-muted max-w-[75ch]">
      Adoption guidance includes phased rollout patterns, ownership frameworks, and governance activation milestones so implementation is
      orderly and measurable. Teams can start with limited scope, validate controls, then expand coverage while maintaining stability.
      This prevents the common failure mode of broad launches without operational readiness. Documentation remains actionable and aligned
      with working reality, which is essential for long-term reliability.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12 rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 grid gap-3">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Need Documentation for Your Environment</h2>
    <p class="text-muted max-w-[72ch]">
      We can tailor docs for your governance, security, and delivery model so teams have clear execution guidance from day one. Call
      <a class="hover:text-text" href="tel:+12136328384"> (213) 632-8384</a> or email
      <a class="hover:text-text" href="mailto:hello@zbestmedia.com"> hello@zbestmedia.com</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a href="/contact" class="rounded-xl bg-gold px-5 py-3 text-bg font-semibold">Book a Strategic Brief</a>
      <a href="/security" class="rounded-xl border border-border px-5 py-3">Review Security Controls</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
